C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\apps\scientific_ui\src\trioctagon_ui\shell_observer.py:161:    """Retain originating request, including raw text and exact/generic authority."""
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:8:\hypersetup{pdftitle={Spherical Edge Extensions of the Folded Tri-Octagon},pdfauthor={Hilmir Frimann Halldorsson},pdfsubject={Proposed v0.1 mathematical synthesis; internal review draft},pdfkeywords={exact geometry, spherical edge extensions, convex hulls, Tri-Octagon}}
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:28:{\Large Exact Incidence Events, Natural Terminations,\par and Convex Envelopes\par}
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:34:Three welded regular octagonal panels determine 21 straight supporting lines. We give a unified account of the accepted BOUNDARY-10, BOUNDARY-11 and BOUNDARY-12 investigations of this fixed arrangement. Every line is tangent at its original edge midpoint to one concentric sphere. Extension to an observer sphere therefore has a common along-line parameter. Exactly three finite radii produce coincidences: each has 18 distinct points carrying 42 branch labels, whereas all other allowed finite radii have 42 distinct positions. The full finite symmetry is exactly $D_{3h}$.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:36:The two nonoriginal events are, on every outward ray, respectively the first subsequent meeting and the last finite meeting with another original line. At the first, the entire clipped segment equals the original panel width; at the last, each endpoint's added extension equals that width. Splitting at all spatial intersections produces three connected frameworks with 21, 63 and 105 edges. Their separately defined convex envelopes have 11, 32 and 20 maximal faces. We give proofs, coordinates, incidence conventions and exact supporting-plane representatives, and explain why the later frameworks lie inside their envelopes instead of tracing their surfaces.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:49:Paper C fixes the welded module and its material indexing \cite{PC}. BOUNDARY-10 identifies a minimum enclosing ball and studies a decagonal comparison \cite{B10}. BOUNDARY-11 classifies the edge-line exits on concentric spheres \cite{B11}. BOUNDARY-12 uses that classification to identify first and last meetings and to construct the finite graphs and their convex hulls \cite{B12}. The two subsequent GPT reviews are separate records \cite{G11,G12}. In particular, the three-term octagonal ratio is attributed to the later BOUNDARY-11 review, not retroactively to the original report.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:52:The \emph{shell} $S$ is the union of the three original filled, zero-thickness octagons. The \emph{framework} $G_k$ consists of clipped straight support lines split at all actual spatial intersections. The \emph{observer sphere} is the level set $\|x\|=R$ in centred coordinates. The \emph{convex envelope} $H_k$ is the convex hull of the unique outer exit points. A proposed physical wall would require additional state, coupling or boundary-condition definitions; it is none of these objects by definition.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:68:The result is finite and exact. One can choose either first or last meeting as an observation preset without changing the panels, and one can display a convex envelope without mistaking it for the straight-line framework. This gives a useful static reference construction even while physical interpretations remain undefined.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:83:$P_k,G_k,H_k$ & Outer points, split graph and convex hull; $k=0,1,2$.\\
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:123:Each panel is the convex hull of its vertices. A closed ball is convex, so the radius-$\rho$ ball contains all three panels. The average of the 18 global vertices is $o$: threefold rotation eliminates the centred horizontal average and horizontal reflection eliminates the vertical average. For an arbitrary proposed ball centre $c$, expand the squares and use $\sum_i(v_i-o)=0$ to obtain
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:229:The complete unique exit set has precisely $D_{3h}$ symmetry, of order 12, at every finite $R\geq\rho$, including all three events.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:232:Rotation by $120^\circ$ about $z$, reflection $x\mapsto-x$, and reflection $z\mapsto-z$ preserve the original edge family and the explicit formulas \eqref{eq:seams}--\eqref{eq:chamfer}. Thus all twelve elements of $D_{3h}$ are present. To exclude more symmetry, first note that the unique finite set has average zero under these existing operations; any isometry preserving the set therefore fixes its centre and is orthogonal.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:234:For a generic 42-point configuration, summing $qq^{\mathsf T}$ over the family formulas gives the accepted exact second moment
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:242:The generic point-orbit sizes are $(6,12,12,12)$. At Original, A and B they are respectively $(6,12)$, $(6,6,6)$ and $(6,12)$. A locally regular polygon or a count of twenty objects does not alter this full spatial group.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:246:Set $f_\pm=(w/2\pm t)/\sqrt2$. The generic signed height levels are $\pm t$ for the seams, $\pm w/2$ for horizontal edges, and $\pm f_+$, $\pm f_-$ for chamfers. There are respectively three, six, six and six points at each signed level. Latitude is $\arcsin(z/R)$. There are generically eight levels, but $f_-$ can be negative, and levels meet at the three incidence events. The middle event includes an equatorial level. Equal height does not itself prove equal position.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:254:Each hexagon's circumradius and chord side are $w/3$. The two $R/w$ displays transcribed from the saved pattern records are \input{tables/hexagon_decimals.tex}. Neither is a global coincidence event: the whole set has 42 points and $D_{3h}$ symmetry. In the allowed range the horizontal-family transverse distance $t$ is too large for this hexagon condition; the other chamfer transverse distance $f_+$ is also too large.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:275:\plate{03_latitude_families}{Signed latitude families as the observer radius varies. These curves evaluate a static coordinate family; they are not trajectories of particles or of model state. Vertical guides mark the three finite coincidence events. The per-level counts are generic and do not count repeated labels as separate event positions. Original BOUNDARY-11 plate, unchanged.}{fig:latitudes}{}
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:344:\section{The three convex envelopes}\label{sec:hulls}
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:352:For $p\in P_k$ and every $x\in P_k$, Cauchy--Schwarz gives $p\cdot x\leq R_k^2$, with equality only for $x=p$ because both have norm $R_k$ and are distinct otherwise. The plane $p\cdot x=R_k^2$ therefore exposes $p$ alone. Every outer point is a vertex; a convex hull of a finite set has no other vertices.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:356:\caption{Maximal coplanar facets, not rendering triangles.}\label{tab:hulls}
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:366:The accepted construction examines each of the $\binom{18}{3}=816$ triples at an event. A noncollinear triple gives a plane with normal $n$ and offset $d$. Proportional equations are merged. Evaluating $n\cdot p-d$ for all outer points in $\mathbb Q(\sqrt2,\sqrt3)$ determines whether the plane supports the set. A mixed sign rejects the plane. Otherwise orient it as $n\cdot x\leq d$, with $d>0$, and retain its entire zero set as one maximal coplanar face.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:368:This is an exhaustive certificate method: every facet of a full-dimensional finite convex hull contains three noncollinear vertices and hence appears among those triples. Conversely, every retained supporting plane with a two-dimensional zero set defines a facet. The saved distinct noncollinear-plane counts are 397, 488 and 397; the retained face counts are 11, 32 and 20. These are accepted enumeration records, not new Stage A executions. Appendix~\ref{app:planes} supplies a compact exact plane description for all 63 facets through their symmetry orbits.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:375:The B trapezoids are isosceles, with three side lengths $w$ and the fourth $(1+\sqrt2/2)w$. Their opposite parallel pair and equal legs are recorded explicitly. The six B rectangles occupy two distinct three-member $D_{3h}$ orbits. The exact squared-side and turn-dot sequences in the face inventories distinguish congruence data from a shared face name. Twenty facets do not make B an icosahedron: its triangles, trapezoids and rectangles are the actual facets.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:377:Every symmetry of $P_k$ acts on $\conv(P_k)$. Conversely every symmetry of $H_k$ permutes its exposed vertex set, exactly $P_k$. The finite point-set theorem therefore proves that each hull's full symmetry is precisely $D_{3h}$, not just that some $D_{3h}$ operations work. The graph is $D_{3h}$-invariant and has that same hull, so its possible symmetry group is bounded in the same way.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:380:\plate{event_networks_and_hulls}{Three connected spatial frameworks and their separately filled convex envelopes. Original edges are dark; added portions retain their edge-family colours; gold denotes actual maximal-face hull edges. Each event view uses its own radius scale. Interior graph crossings are spatial only when present in the exact split inventory. Transparent facets express convexification, not an existing material wall. Original BOUNDARY-12 plate, unchanged.}{fig:graphs}{}
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:382:\section{Where the framework lies, and why the envelopes are nested}\label{sec:locus}
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:391:\caption{Framework placement in its corresponding convex envelope.}\label{tab:locus}
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:404:For $t_j<t_k$, the point $m_e+t_j u_e$ is the convex combination of $m_e+t_k u_e$ and $m_e-t_k u_e$ with weights $(1+t_j/t_k)/2$ and $(1-t_j/t_k)/2$. The analogous relation holds for the minus branch. Thus every earlier outer point belongs to a later clipped segment, and hence to the later hull. Taking convex hulls proves nesting. Similarity preserves polyhedral edge and facet incidence; the three distinct edge/facet counts in Table~\ref{tab:hulls} exclude mutual similarity. The inclusions are proper, since otherwise the hulls and their extreme sets would agree.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:406:This distinction makes a practical observation choice possible: a viewer can show the connected line network and the envelope as separate layers, without treating either as a new law of evolution.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:442:The controls separate $R/w$, $C/w$ and $\ell/w$ through the shared along-line parameter. Units, original panels, added segments, reference spheres, split graph and convex envelope have distinct roles. Graph and hull overlays are defined only at the three exact events. Rendering may triangulate a maximal face internally, but the gold edges show the polygon boundary rather than fan diagonals. The exporter retains all 42 branch records separately from the unique-dot identities. Numeric proximity alone does not certify an event.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:484:The fixed welded module supplies a unique minimum enclosing ball and a smaller edge-tangent sphere. The tangent-line structure makes all support-line exits share one parameter. The finite coincidence classification is complete, so first and last meetings are well-defined on every outward ray. Two distinct unit-length statements follow exactly. The split frameworks are connected; their separately defined convex hulls are closed surfaces with certified polygon facets. Nesting does not make them similar, and extending a framework does not fill its envelope.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:518:Use centred coordinates and width-normalized $X=x/w$. Every representative in Table~\ref{tab:planes} is the inequality $n\cdot X\leq d$. Apply the twelve operations generated by rotation through $120^\circ$, $x\mapsto-x$ and $z\mapsto-z$, and remove duplicate planes. The orbit sizes give all maximal faces. Plane normals are not required to be unit vectors; scaling $n,d$ together does not change a halfspace.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS\publication\v0.1\SPHERICAL_EDGE_EXTENSIONS_v0.1.tex:538:Its prism over $|z|\leq w/2$ contains the shell exactly when it contains the three seam footprints. Necessity holds because those footprints belong to the shell; sufficiency follows from linearity on their convex hull $K$. For fixed orientation the minimum apothem is therefore
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_REPORT.md:9:The three split spatial graphs are connected, with `(V,E,cycle rank)` equal to **(18,21,4)**, **(36,63,28)** and **(54,105,52)**. Their separate convex hulls have `(V,E,F)` equal to **(18,27,11)**, **(18,48,32)** and **(18,36,20)**. At the original sphere all 21 graph edges lie on hull edges. At A and B, the relative interior of **every** split graph edge is strictly inside the hull; only the 18 outer graph vertices lie on its boundary. Filling a surface is a separate construction.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_REPORT.md:13:![Three separate event graphs and convex envelopes](figures/event_networks_and_hulls.png)
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_REPORT.md:109:## 4. Convex envelopes: complete supporting-plane certificates
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_REPORT.md:115:| Event | V | E | F | Maximal face types |
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_REPORT.md:123:**Finite exact construction and completeness.** For each of the `C(18,3)=816` triples, form the cross-product normal n of its affine plane. Discard collinear triples and merge proportional plane equations. Evaluate all 18 residuals `n·p−d` in `Q(√2,√3)`. A plane is retained precisely when all residuals have the same weak sign; orient it to `n·x≤d` with `d>0`. Its complete zero set is the maximal coplanar face. Every facet of a full-dimensional finite convex hull contains three noncollinear vertices, so this exhaustive enumeration omits no facet.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_REPORT.md:125:For a compact independently usable description, the following table lists one outward plane `n·(x/w)≤d` per D3h face orbit. Apply rotations by 120 degrees about z, reflection `x→−x`, and reflection `z→−z`, removing duplicate planes. Coordinates are centred. The orbit sizes sum to the face counts above.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_REPORT.md:143:For each retained face, all residuals, exact vertices, cyclic order, side metrics and orientation are in `data/hull_EVENT.json`. Numerical atan2 only nominated a cyclic order. Every directed side was then checked exactly to put all other face vertices on its inward side, which certifies the convex polygon order; the polygon's outward turn is also positive. The face plane has precisely the stated zero-set vertices, so adjacent coplanar triangles are not counted as separate faces.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_REPORT.md:147:**Symmetry.** D3h acts on the accepted point sets and therefore on their hulls. Conversely every hull symmetry permutes its exposed vertices, so it is a symmetry of P_k. Accepted BOUNDARY-11 proves the full finite point-set group is exactly D3h; no larger hull group is possible. The new face-orbit tables independently verify the facet action. The graph has the same group: it is D3h-invariant, and its convex hull is H_k, which bounds its possible spatial symmetries.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_REPORT.md:159:The three envelopes are nested because an earlier point lies on a segment between later exits of its own original line. They are **not mutually similar**, as their face/edge counts differ. This matters for the scaling discussion below.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_REPORT.md:260:| R/w = 10; R/w = T | conventional unit/parameter | Two prescribed observation comparisons; generic 42-point cases. BOUNDARY12 work order §5 C3. |
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_REPORT.md:300:The new exact construction passes 441 named predicates, including all three supporting-plane inventories, oriented maximal faces, manifold links, graph connectivity and source containment. The additional written-data certificate verifies exactly which graph vertices are strictly interior. The new viewer checker passes 114 deterministic checks; its largest JavaScript/Python coordinate discrepancy across the seven prescribed cases is 1.78e-15 in width units. These are deterministic finite checks, not stochastic trials. Source PDF pages and both static figure sets were visually inspected. Actual browser actions and screenshots are recorded in `evidence/BROWSER_QA.json`; final source hashes and execution receipts accompany this report.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_REPORT.md:310:- `data/hull_original.json`, `hull_A.json`, `hull_B.json`: exact maximal faces, supporting-plane residuals, orientation, edges, metrics and symmetry orbits.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\future\OPEN_QUESTIONS.md:9:| PHY01 | A geometric envelope is not yet a physical or material boundary | Define the field/state, a coupling or boundary condition, units and an explicit testable effect before adding forces/reflections. | No; separate modeling decision. |
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary10\BOUNDARY10_REPORT.tex:25:Paper C v1.0.1 [C, \S\S2--9] fixes three regular octagonal panels of flat-to-flat width $w>0$, edge $s=(\sqrt2-1)w$, folded at $60^\circ$. Their welded surface $S$ has 18 vertices, three faces, and two \emph{nonplanar} nine-edge rims. It is an annulus, with symmetry $D_{3h}$; it does not bound a solid by itself. Its axial projection is the perimeter of an equilateral triangle $K$ of side $w$, and $|z|\leq w/2$.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary10\BOUNDARY10_REPORT.tex:64:The angle difference is $9^\circ$. For \emph{co-centred, coplanar} aligned direction grids, $45^\circ=5(9^\circ)$ and $36^\circ=4(9^\circ)$ generate a 40-direction refinement; their common rotations form $C_2$. This is arithmetic, not a new folded-shell symmetry. In particular, the shell's $60^\circ$ fold and $120^\circ$ normal separation are not polygon exterior turns.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary10\BOUNDARY10_REPORT.tex:75:\tagline{New enclosure consequence, with proof.} The closed ball contains every panel: each panel is the convex hull of its vertices and a ball is convex. The vertex average is $o$ by the threefold and horizontal-reflection symmetries. For any proposed centre $c$,
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary10\BOUNDARY10_REPORT.tex:81:Two other exact constructions are useful. The solid $K\times[-w/2,w/2]$ is a triangular-prism envelope fixed by the known projection and height. The convex hull $\conv(S)$ is the unique smallest \emph{convex set} containing the shell; choosing convexity is a convention, though the hull then needs no free parameter. Neither is tenfold. These envelopes contain $S$ but touch it; strictly positive clearance requires an additional specified distance. They are mathematical reference constructions, not added kernel surfaces or material caps.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary10\BOUNDARY10_REPORT.tex:100:This is necessary because the shell contains the three seam footprints. It is sufficient because all projected shell points lie on $\partial K$, and linear inequalities holding at the three corners hold throughout their convex hull $K$.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\accepted\boundary10\BOUNDARY10_REPORT.tex:146:\textbf{Recommended discussion candidate:} the unique minimum enclosing sphere, if the aim is a neutral outer spatial reference; $\conv(S)$ if a tight convex polyhedral envelope is preferred. Retain the centred decagonal prism only as an explicitly optional tenfold comparison with its orientation choice disclosed. No evidence here selects tenfold closure as intrinsic, or identifies $\Delta$ with outside matter, energy or a physical field.
