C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\reviews\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_GPT_SCIENTIFIC_REVIEW.md:6:**Mathematical verdict:** **ACCEPTED_WITHIN_STATIC_TERMINATION_GRAPH_AND_CONVEX_ENVELOPE_SCOPE**  
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\reviews\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_GPT_SCIENTIFIC_REVIEW.md:30:Exact arithmetic is in Q(sqrt(2),sqrt(3)). All zero and sign decisions for hull support are made on these exact algebraic values. The review independently enumerated all 816 vertex triples at each event, merging proportional plane equations and retaining precisely the supporting planes. It verified all maximal coplanar vertex sets, their outward orientations, side-squared and turn-dot sequences, actual edge incidences, closed vertex links, containment and D3h facet orbits.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\reviews\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_GPT_SCIENTIFIC_REVIEW.md:32:| Event | Split graph V/E | Components | Cycle rank | Hull V/E/F | Maximal face types |
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\reviews\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_GPT_SCIENTIFIC_REVIEW.md:38:Across the three hulls there are 2,448 examined vertex triples and 63 maximal facets. The numbers of distinct noncollinear planes are 397, 488 and 397. Face orbit sizes are respectively (2,3,6), (2,6,6,6,12), and (2,3,3,6,6).
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\reviews\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_GPT_SCIENTIFIC_REVIEW.md:42:Each hull's exposed vertex set is exactly the corresponding 18 outer sphere points. Consequently the accepted upper bound on point-set symmetry also bounds the hull's symmetry. The graph has that hull and is D3h invariant, giving the same complete spatial symmetry. No additional global tenfold symmetry follows from a face or vertex count.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\reviews\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_GPT_SCIENTIFIC_REVIEW.md:54:At A and B every open segment is interior, even though some endpoints touch the hull. Thus extending the lines constructs a connected interior framework; it does not itself trace or fill the hull's outer faces. The convex hull is a separately declared, parameter-free enclosure once the point set and convexification convention are chosen.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\reviews\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_GPT_SCIENTIFIC_REVIEW.md:56:The original convex hull adds six boundary edges and eight faces beyond the existing three-panel shell. None of these new filled faces is retrospectively part of Paper C.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\reviews\boundary12\BOUNDARY12_GPT_SCIENTIFIC_REVIEW.md:118:The BOUNDARY-10/11/12 sequence now supports a detailed mathematical account and a useful observation-only application feature. Recommended next preparation is a unified account of the edge-tangent sphere, finite incidence events, natural clipping lengths, connected frameworks, and their separately defined convex envelopes. A later scoped app task should first inspect the actual integration boundary, handle V01, and prove observation leaves kernel state, random state and time unchanged.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\GEOMETRIC_ORIGINS_AND_NORMALIZATION\publication\v0.2\GEO_ORIGINS01_v0.2.tex:21:The folded Tri-Octagon has an exact geometry, while the images that motivated its construction have often been less explicit in the written record. This companion places the author's retrospective account beside the surviving definitions and accepted results. Opposite signs express an imagined other side; a unit expresses a normalization; neither alone specifies a physical sphere. The native shell nevertheless has a unique minimum enclosing ball. Extending its original straight lines gives two natural events: a whole unit-width chord at Crossing A and a unit-width addition at each endpoint at Crossing B. A also circumscribes the earlier measurement cylinder and triangular-prism envelope, and its endpoint addition equals the shortest native notch gap. We trace the historical resonance-lock proposal through the fixed target $\gamma_*$ and the exact golden-interval zeta identity. These connections are retained without conflating layer counts, analytic coefficients, assigned spatial units and an as-yet unspecified structural extent.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\apps\scientific_ui\publication\shell_observer\v0.1\README.md:5:Original, Crossing A and Crossing B select the accepted exact events. A has whole chord C/w = 1; B has added extension ell/w = 1 at each endpoint. The panel retains all 42 edge-branch identities. Exact-event incidence networks and their convex hulls are separate optional layers. Generic observations preserve branch identities without inferring coincidences, event topology or a certified distinct-position count. Assigned radius comparisons are comparison scales, not physical boundaries.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\GEOMETRIC_ORIGINS_AND_NORMALIZATION\publication\v0.2\retained_mathematics.tex:48:The three filled, zero-thickness panels form the native shell $S$. Their edges are finite segments. Their infinite supporting lines form another object. A sphere of radius $R$ clips those lines into a framework, and the convex hull of their outer exits forms an envelope. An observation surface is not a new panel, and an envelope does not supply a material wall. These distinctions give the construction image a precise vocabulary without requiring a physical interpretation.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\GEOMETRIC_ORIGINS_AND_NORMALIZATION\publication\v0.2\retained_mathematics.tex:61:An enclosing ball must have squared radius at least this mean. The centre $c=0$ attains $\rho$; every other centre requires a larger radius. Convexity of a ball extends containment from vertices to each panel, all finite edges and their convex hull. This restates the BOUNDARY-10 proof retained in the accepted synthesis.~\cite[Sec.~3]{SP}
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\GEOMETRIC_ORIGINS_AND_NORMALIZATION\publication\v0.2\retained_mathematics.tex:96:\caption{Accepted event geometry at common width $w$; decimals are rounded. A framework includes interior intersections; its envelope is a separate convex polyhedron.}\label{tab:events}
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\GEOMETRIC_ORIGINS_AND_NORMALIZATION\publication\v0.2\retained_mathematics.tex:112:The triangular-prism envelope $K\times[-w/2,w/2]$ has six corners with that same radius and mean zero. The mean-square argument in \eqref{eq:minball} forces its unique minimum ball; the ball also contains the whole cylinder, whose rim circles attain its radius. The six prism corners are precisely the A exits of the three seam lines. This is the accepted cross-source connection between measurement geometry and first line meetings.~\cite{TSZSphere,TSZReview}
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\GEOMETRIC_ORIGINS_AND_NORMALIZATION\publication\v0.2\retained_mathematics.tex:189:Solving for a target uses that target as input; it does not predict $T$. The accepted incidence exhaustion already implies that this radius beyond B is a generic forty-two-point observation, with no later finite supporting-line meeting. B remains the event for which $\ell_B=w$.~\cite{TSZZeta,SP}
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\GEOMETRIC_ORIGINS_AND_NORMALIZATION\publication\v0.2\retained_mathematics.tex:198:The historical constant chain is also substantial: a proposed lock, a clearly defined target, an exact golden-interval integral and a separately normalized spectral construction. They explain why $T$, $\ph$ and ten were meaningful questions before the present radius comparison. They leave open the map from a proposed total structural extent to a specified length, area, volume or tensor norm. If $T$ and ten measured the same quantity on similar objects, their \emph{linear} scale ratio would be $T/10$ for length, $\sqrt{T/10}$ for area, or $(T/10)^{1/3}$ for volume. That conditional conversion does not make the three event envelopes similar or define a tensor norm.~\cite[Sec.~14]{SP}
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\reviews\boundary11\BOUNDARY11_GPT_SCIENTIFIC_REVIEW.md:71:The three D3h generators preserve the complete symbolic branch family. The generic second moment is exactly
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\research\spherical_edge_extensions\reviews\boundary11\BOUNDARY11_GPT_SCIENTIFIC_REVIEW.md:77:At each exceptional radius, the unique-point second moment instead has vertical-minus-horizontal difference 3w²/2. Together with the three-point seam level, this establishes the report's upper bound on possible symmetries, not merely the presence of some D3h operations. The exact finite group remains D3h throughout R>=rho. The event orbit sizes are (6,12), (6,6,6), and (6,12).
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:7:Authoritative construction input: `TRIOCTAGON_ACTUAL_GEOMETRY_SPEC_v0.2.md` and the width-1 verification package `verification/actual_geometry/width_1/`. The analytic results below are **supported** by `verification/paperC/paperC_geometry_audit_v0_2_1.py`, which executes **51 symbolic/combinatorial regression predicates across all 19 numbered coverage groups** the referee identified (no tautological or expected-minus-itself checks). These finite checks **supplement, but do not replace, the analytic proofs**: this is an execution count of finite predicates, not a count of independent theorems, and the general-angle closure solution family and the full $D_{3h}$ upper bound remain analytic proofs (§§5, 9). See §10 and the companion `PAPER_C_PROOF_AUDIT_v0.2.2.md`, which separates analytic proof from executed regression from preserved historical validation.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:13:Three identical regular octagons, each normalized to flat-to-flat width $1$ (edge $s=\sqrt2-1$), admit an exact symmetric folded realization. The middle panel is held fixed; the two outer panels are attached along the middle panel's vertical hinge edges in a reversed local orientation and rotated by a fold magnitude $\beta=60^\circ$ about those hinges. The two free outer-panel edges then coincide, completing a third seam alongside the two prescribed hinges and producing a welded lateral shell with $V=18$ vertices, $E=21$ edges, and $F=3$ octagonal faces, Euler characteristic $\chi=0$, one connected component, three vertical seam edges, and **two open nine-edge boundary rims** ($9s+9s$). The shell is a connected, compact, orientable $2$-manifold with boundary and two boundary components; applying $\chi=2-2g-b$ gives $g=0$, so it is an **annulus (a sphere with two disjoint open discs removed)**, **not** a punctured torus — the vanishing $\chi$ alone does not classify it, and the boundary is the deciding datum. Every horizontal cross-section through the central band ($|z|\le s/2$) meets the shell in the **perimeter** of an equilateral triangle of side exactly $1$ (the enclosed planar region, to which area/inradius/circumradius belong, is that filled triangle). The interior dihedral along each hinge is $60^\circ$ and the angle between outward face normals is $120^\circ$. The twelve chamfer boundary edges have exact $45^\circ$ inclination to the horizontal, while the panel planes themselves are vertical. The spatial notch at each rim tip has tip angle $\arccos(3/4)\approx41.4096^\circ$, distinct from its axial projection, which is equilateral and therefore $60^\circ$. The exact Euclidean symmetry group of the welded shell surface is $D_{3h}$ (order $12$). All coordinates and derived quantities are available in closed form.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:33:The general tools used here are standard, and we cite them as background rather than as new results. Regular-polygon metric relations are elementary Euclidean geometry [Cox73, Cox69]; folding rigid polygonal panels about hinge/crease lines is the setting of geometric folding and rigid-origami theory [DO07, Tachi09]; the object is a polyhedral (piecewise-linear) surface with boundary [RS72]; the Euler characteristic and the classification of compact orientable surfaces with boundary via $\chi=2-2g-b$ are standard topology [Massey91, GX13, Hatcher02]; the interior-dihedral / outward-normal angle conventions are standard Euclidean geometry [Cox73], and the Schoenflies point group $D_{3h}\cong D_3\times C_s$ is standard point-group theory [Cotton90, AH94]; and exact/robust arithmetic for geometric predicates is the exact-computation paradigm of computational geometry [Yap97, KMPSY08, Shewchuk97]. In every case the cited source supplies the *general* framework, terminology, or theorem; the hypotheses, coordinates, and results **for this specific surface are established here**.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:37:The contribution is **not** any of the standard background just listed — not the regular-octagon formulas, the Euler characteristic, the surface-classification theorem, the vertex-link manifold criterion, the dihedral-angle convention, $D_{3h}$ group theory, or the exact-arithmetic methodology. The model-specific contribution is the **exact closed-form construction and complete geometric characterization of this particular three-octagon polyhedral surface**, namely: the $18$ exact welded vertices (§4); the general-$\beta$ closure relation and its $\beta=\tfrac{\pi}{3}$ solution (§5); the topology and annulus classification *for this surface*, with its hypotheses verified (§5); the exact equilateral central section (§6); the exact interior-dihedral and outward-normal values (§7); the chamfer-edge geometry (§6); the spatial and projected notch geometry (§8); the rim/hexagon decomposition (§8); and the proof that the exact Euclidean symmetry group of this surface is $D_{3h}$, with a matching upper bound (§9). A focused literature search did not locate a close precedent for this specific three-octagon polyhedral surface; its novelty is therefore left unresolved and **no priority claim is made**.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:81:1. **Stacked-flat reference.** All three panels start coplanar in the plane $y=0$. The middle panel keeps its local orientation, $P_2^{0}(u,z)=(u,\,0,\,z)$. The two outer panels start in the **reversed local-$u$** orientation, $P_1^{0}(u,z)=P_3^{0}(u,z)=(-u,\,0,\,z)$; i.e. each outer panel is laid over the middle panel with its local $u$-axis reversed. (This reversed start is what makes the subsequent fold magnitude $60^\circ$; see §7.)
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:223:- **Fold magnitude $\beta=60^\circ$.** This is the rotation applied to each outer panel about its hinge, measured from the **reversed stacked-flat start** (§3). The reversed start places the outer panel's far-edge direction antiparallel to the middle panel (at $180^\circ$ in the $xy$-plane, relative to the outward middle direction); a rotation of $\beta=60^\circ$ brings it to the closed apex. Equivalently, measured from the *unfolded coplanar* configuration (outer panel co-directional with the middle panel, at $0^\circ$), the rotation about the hinge is $180^\circ-60^\circ=120^\circ$. The magnitude "$\pm60^\circ$" names the fold from the reversed start, and this is why the reversed local-$u$ orientation is part of the construction.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:289:$D_{3h}$ is a standard Schoenflies point group: order $12$, with a $C_3$ principal axis, three $C_2'$ axes, three vertical mirror planes $\sigma_v$, a horizontal mirror plane $\sigma_h$, and two $S_3$ operations, and $D_{3h}\cong D_3\times C_s$ [Cotton90, AH94]. That is the group's standard description and terminology. The theorem below is the separate, model-specific statement that **this particular surface realizes exactly $D_{3h}$**; it is proved here by a lower bound (twelve exhibited symmetries) and a matching upper bound, not quoted from the group tables.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:291:> **Theorem 6 (symmetry group).** The Euclidean symmetry group of the unlabelled welded surface $S$ (equivalently of its geometric face complex) is $\operatorname{Sym}(S)=D_3\times C_s\cong D_{3h}$, of order $12$.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:293:**Proof — lower bound (twelve symmetries).** Three generators, each an isometry mapping the three octagonal **faces** (each the convex hull of its vertex cycle) onto faces:
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:297:Since each generator maps each octagon's vertex cycle onto another octagon's vertex cycle and a face is the convex hull of its cycle, the generators map **whole faces** to faces (and edges to edges). $\langle C_3,\sigma_v\rangle\cong D_3$ (order $6$); adjoining $\sigma_h$, which commutes with both, gives $D_3\times\{E,\sigma_h\}=D_{3h}$ of order $12$, elements $\{E,2C_3,3\sigma_v,\sigma_h,2S_3,3C_2'\}$, with $S_3=\sigma_h C_3$.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:301:\operatorname{Sym}(S)=D_3\times C_s\cong D_{3h},\qquad |\operatorname{Sym}(S)|=12. \qquad\blacksquare
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:304:This is the symmetry of the unlabelled surface/face complex (panel permutation allowed), not a claim that material labels are fixed. The **finite 18-vertex set** has the same group here, but by its own argument, not automatically: its centroid is $O_c$ and its covariance matrix in centred coordinates is $\operatorname{diag}\bigl(\tfrac{1+s^2}{12},\tfrac{1+s^2}{12},\tfrac{2+s^2}{12}\bigr)$, whose distinct vertical eigenvalue forces every vertex-set symmetry to preserve the vertical axis; the six vertices at $|z|=\tfrac{s}{2}$ (footprints $C_1,C_2,C_3$) then give the same $6\times2$ bound, and the twelve maps realize it. The group is $D_{3h}$, not a higher cyclic or dihedral group, and specifically **not** the historical $D_{24}$ (see §10). The independent audit confirms $C_3^3=\mathrm{id}$ pointwise, $C_3\ne\mathrm{id}$, $\sigma_v^2=\sigma_h^2=\mathrm{id}$, the conjugation $\sigma_vC_3\sigma_v=C_3^{-1}$, the commutation $\sigma_hC_3=C_3\sigma_h$, and the face/edge permutation action (§10, Appendix C).
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:329:Every predicate executes a real computation on reconstructed data — **no literal-`True` and no expected-minus-itself checks**. The suite does **not** by itself prove the complete closure family, the quantified all-$z$ section / maximal-band statement, the interior-dihedral theorem, the boundary sign separation, the chamfer-edge theorem, or the full $D_{3h}$ upper bound; each of those is the analytic argument of §§5–9 (finite sampling does not prove them). The v0.1 harness, by contrast, advertised "46/46 independent checks" but contained $3$ tautological and $10$ weak predicates and left $19$ coverage groups untested; that claim is withdrawn.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:334:- Concentric three-octagon "$\mathbb Z_{24}$/$D_{24}$" pictures are *not* this welded shell; they describe a different (nested, planar) arrangement and a different symmetry label. The symmetry group established here is $D_{3h}$ (§9).
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:348:Three regular octagonal panels, each normalized to flat-to-flat width $1$ (edge $s=\sqrt2-1$), admit an exact symmetric folded realization whose welded lateral shell has $V=18$, $E=21$, $F=3$, Euler characteristic $0$, one connected component, three vertical seams, and **two open nine-edge boundaries** ($9s$ each). As a connected, compact, orientable $2$-manifold with boundary and two boundary components, it has genus $0$ and is an **annulus — a sphere with two disjoint open discs removed** — not a closed polyhedron and not a (punctured) torus: the boundary is the deciding datum. Every horizontal section through the central band meets the shell in the **perimeter** of an equilateral triangle of side exactly $1$ (area, inradius, and circumradius belong to the enclosed filled triangle, not to the section curve); the interior dihedral is $60^\circ$ and the outward-normal separation is $120^\circ$; the twelve chamfer boundary edges have exact $45^\circ$ inclination to the horizontal while the panel planes are vertical; the spatial notch angle is $\arccos(3/4)\approx41.4096^\circ$, distinct from its equilateral $60^\circ$ axial projection; and the exact Euclidean symmetry group of the surface is $D_{3h}$, established with both a twelve-element realization and a matching upper bound. All coordinates and derived angles are available in closed form. The analytic results are supported by $51$ executed symbolic/combinatorial regression predicates spanning $19$ numbered coverage groups; these finite checks supplement, but do not replace, the analytic proofs. No physical interpretation is claimed.
C:\TORMENT\TRIOCTAGON_new\research\TRIOCTAGON_OUTER_ENVELOPE_01\run_20261113_01\sources\papers\PAPER_C\publication\v1.0.1\PAPER_C_EXACT_TRIOCTAGON_GEOMETRY_PUBLICATION_v1.0.1.md:389:`verification/paperC/paperC_geometry_audit_v0_2_1.py` (pure sympy; no source, kernel, or builder import) reconstructs the shell from the octagon and the **general-angle** fold transforms and executes **51 regression predicates across all 19 numbered coverage groups** (§10), all PASS, including the regression against the authoritative welded coordinate table. Every predicate is a real computation on reconstructed data (no literal-`True`, no expected-minus-itself); nested-radical equalities use sympy's exact symbolic prover. The count is an execution count of finite symbolic/combinatorial predicates that **supplement** the analytic proofs — it is **not** a count of independent theorems. Several results are owned by the analytic arguments, not by these finite checks: the complete closure solution family (§5), the quantified all-$z$ section and maximal-band statement (§6), the interior-dihedral relation (§7), the boundary sign separation (§5), the chamfer-edge inclination (§6), and the full-group $D_{3h}$ upper bound (§9). v0.2.1 changed only two predicate names and several descriptions from v0.2; the predicate logic is unchanged. The original `paperC_geometry_audit.py` (v0.1) and `paperC_geometry_audit_v0_2.py` (v0.2) are preserved unchanged.
